{:en {:title "Kotoba native performance: Embench at 0.821× C time with same-algorithm kernels, 15 October 2026"
:description "A corrected, independently verified full 19-workload round on an Apple M4: with every algorithm-substituting kernel removed and no crypto instructions, translated Kotoba programs ran in 0.821× of C's execution time (geometric mean). Eight workloads are still slower than C."
:intro "This is the corrected result. After the 0.800× round earlier the same day, an audit found certified kernels that substituted the benchmark's algorithm. We removed or rewrote every one of them so that each kernel performs the program's own work, turned off the SHA-256 instructions, and measured again. Kotoba time / C is 0.8213 in geometric mean (0.821×). Eleven workloads run faster than their C versions; eight are still slower. This round replaces the 0.800× figure."
:notice "These are measurements of translated Kotoba programs using the upstream Embench speed driver and formula, with a revised per-workload iteration count. They are not a certified official Kotoba Embench score, a released-toolchain result, or a claim of speed on other hosts or other programs."
:sections [{:title "Result: 0.821× C time across all 19 workloads"
:paragraphs ["The round accepted all 19 Kotoba and all 19 C samples, each on its first attempt, with no CPU-condition retries. The normalized geometric mean of Kotoba time / C is 0.821298642657085. Driver geometric scores are 43297.65 for translated Kotoba and 35560.30 for original C (higher is faster)."
"Every Kotoba sample matched the result, benchmark fuel, verification fuel and all 17 arena counters expected at its iteration count. Those values were re-derived from trusted reference images (the round-51 originals, cross-checked against round-114 images), not from the compiler being measured. All 19 C samples were exact. An offline verifier recomputed every ratio from the raw records and passed."
"The measured compiler is source commit a9edc1f0d5021efabf6864e70c5b92d59257f348 of kotoba-lang/amu, published on a branch and not merged into main. Rebuilding it from amu's public bootstrap reaches the measured binary (sha256 e5fc0ca2…) as a fixed point: G1 = G2 = G3. That rebuilt compiler produces the 19 timed images byte for byte. Before measurement the compiler passed 39 image compiles, the 483 result/fuel/state observations and a 71,496-case differential test."]}
{:title "Kernel scope: same algorithm only"
:paragraphs ["A certified kernel is hand-written AArch64 that the compiler emits when a function's intermediate code exactly matches a recognized shape, with fuel accounting identical to the general path and a fallback to it. Under the rule used for this round, a kernel may change how a function executes: instruction selection, scheduling, unrolling, SIMD, register-held copies of the program's data, hoisted checks, reassociation of integer reductions, dead-store elimination, merging identical reads, repacking the function's own constant tables at compile time, and fusing consecutive loops without violating dependences. It may not change what work is executed. Closed forms, simulation on a copy, new run-time data structures, reuse across repetitions and different algorithms are not allowed, and unclear cases count as not allowed."
"Every kernel in the compiler was audited against that rule, and the full tables are published with this report. The substituting kernels found in huffbench, qrduino, tarfind, ud, picojpeg, sglib and xgboost were removed or rewritten to perform the source's own steps. nettle-sha256 now runs a scalar kernel instead of the SHA-256 instructions. Admission is still by code shape, never by workload name, but the kernels were written for hot functions of these ports, so their speed does not carry over to other programs automatically."]}
{:title "All 19 workloads"
:paragraphs ["Each ratio is Kotoba time per iteration / C time per iteration; below 1.000× means Kotoba is faster. m is the Kotoba iteration multiplier. The m93 column is the earlier 0.800× round. Rows marked * used substituting kernels or the SHA-256 instructions in m93, so those m93 values are not same-algorithm comparisons."]
