:caption "Geometric mean 0.821×. Eight workloads remain slower than C. Single measurement cycle on one Apple M4 host; C itself ran 4.4% slower in geometric mean than in the m93 cycle."}}
{:title "Protocol"
:paragraphs ["The protocol is the one used for the 0.800× round: each Kotoba workload runs LSF × 8192 × m iterations, with m (1, 2, 4, 8 or 16) fixed in a hashed pre-registration before transfer, and the ratio is (4 / m) × Kotoba ns / C ns. Unchanged: the C arm and its frozen binaries, the 0.5 s floor, the 2^35 fuel budget, the trusted loader, one warmup and one accepted sample, at most three CPU-only attempts, and taking the first qualified sample rather than the fastest."
"Two amendments were recorded before any timing. First, an image byte-identical to one timed earlier may be timed again inside a complete, pre-registered round with fresh C, and is disclosed. aha-mont64's image is identical to the earlier round's and can only run at m = 1 because of the loader's vector cap; it took 2.039 s now against 1.953 s then (×1.044), the same drift as the C arm in this cycle. Second, a workload already at its largest feasible m is accepted with a projected interval of at least 0.6 s instead of 0.85 s; this applied to matmult-int (projected 0.77 s, measured 0.814 s). Every interval cleared the 0.5 s floor."]}
{:title "How to verify"
:paragraphs ["The reproduction package in kotoba-lang/amu (branch claude/embench-same-algo-m104-reproduce-20261015, bench/embench/reproduce-20261015) carries this round's configuration, pins and records. Level (a) recomputes every ratio and the geometric mean from the published raw records and checks all hashes, without Apple hardware. Level (b) rebuilds the compiler from amu's public bootstrap to the measured fixed point and recompiles the 19 ports to byte-identical images. Level (c) runs every image at its iteration count against the trusted-reference values. Level (d) runs a same-host Kotoba and C timing campaign. Levels (a) to (c) passed from a fresh clone. The same branch still reproduces the earlier 0.800× round."]}
{:title "Measurement method"
:paragraphs ["Both arms ran on the same Apple M4 Mac mini in the same cycle, Kotoba first and then fresh C. The C arm uses Apple clang 17.0.0 with -O2 -std=gnu11 and unchanged binaries frozen in round 65. Upstream Embench revision 09c2ed8c3b7008c95d08b038de4a3f6dc103ed70 supplies the untouched speed driver and score formula. CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW brackets the workload body; warmup, resets and verification run outside it. Admission requires at least 70% raw CPU idle before and after each sample and at least 70% background idle; the host's load gate read 90.6% median and 89.4% minimum idle before the round."]}
{:title "What is still slower than C"
:paragraphs ["xgboost (1.518×), statemate (1.436×), picojpeg (1.407×), qrduino (1.209×), slre (1.169×), huffbench (1.159×), sglib-combined (1.156×) and nettle-sha256 (1.009×) are slower than C. Work on qrduino, huffbench, picojpeg, slre and the compiler's generic loop optimizations is continuing under the same-algorithm rule; it is not part of this result."]}]
